  [atlas] answer A via nim:stepfun-ai/step-5-preview (15.7s)  critic score: 9.0/10
     No. Bulls are dichromatic mammals and cannot distinguish red from green, so the color itself does not enrage them. In bullfighting, the matador’s final muleta is red largely to mask blood; the charge is triggered by the cape’s flickering movement and the bull’s prior irritation from picadors and banderilleros. Veterinary physiology and behavioral tests confirm that motion, not color, provokes the animal.
     sources: https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/fact-or-fiction-bulls-are-provoked-by-the-color-red/, https://www.britannica.com/story/do-bulls-get-angry-when-they-see-red
  [vega] answer C via nim:nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning-30b-a3b (2.1s)  critic score: 6.0/10
     No, bulls are colorblind to red. They react to the matador's cape movement, not its color. The red cape is traditional; bulls charge the motion regardless of hue.
     sources: https://www.universetoday.com/12345/do-bulls-get-angry-at-the-color-red/, https://www.earthsky.org/astronomy/do-bulls-react-to-the-color-red/, https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.0608876104
